      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"tribal\" element you refer to is central in Central Africa, where politics, resources, and identity often intrinsically overlap, unlike in highly institutionalized Europe or East Asia. In densely populated regions like yours, control over state functions (politics) and lucrative assets (mining) can quickly trigger zero-sum ethnicор conceptions when one group feels marginalized by the other. This makes stability not just a political failure but a structural feature of the local environment, requiring models that factor in ethnic bargaining pressures rather than just standard sovereign risk.\n\nBecause resources and land are finite, multi-ethnic setups in this belt inherently face higher friction when external actors enter, as the group dynamics become more rigid and defensive. Unlike homogenous regions where national consensus can usually override local disputes, here competing identities often mobilize rapidly around grievances, creating a volatility loop that disrupts infrastructure timelines. Therefore, your risk model must explicitly account for the potential for violent exploitation of these identity fractures during periods of political transition or economic fluctuation.",
